### Page 14 of 16

```text
The complete MPO plan is attached to this staff report as Exhibit “B”.
In March of 2018, Mayor Orr transferred the parking administration management to the Cheyenne Police
Department.  The Cheyenne Police Department was already involved in the establishment of an RFP to
automate parking management and enforcement.  The Cheyenne Police Department reviewed the MPO Plan
and started assessing what actions needed to be accomplished.  These ordinance amendments are products of
this review, while assessing the needs of the Cheyenne Community.
In October of 2019, the Cheyenne Police Department activated the automated parking system.  This not only
included the parking structures but also on-street parking enforcement.  Which is ahead of schedule.
ANALYSIS
The proposed changes will provide a more efficient and manageable parking program that will address chronic
abuses and loopholes within the existing system.  The ordinance amendments are intended to promote
compliance with City parking ordinances and to improve the overall public parking experience. Existing codes
rely on inefficient processes and the updates are intended to introduce customer conveniences and automation.
Some of the recommended changes will optimize compliance, monitoring and convenience.  For example,
Chapter 8.40, Food and Food Service Establishments, Chapter 10.52.230, Downtown construction/maintenance
worker parking, Chapter 10.68.04, Residential parking permits authority and Chapter 10.68.080, Downtown
residential parking.  This special permit addition will allow businesses or individuals to park longer than the
two-hour time limitation.  These special permits are not required but allow business or individuals added
convenience.  This will also give the Traffic Engineer additional information about traffic flow and parking
availability.  They will know parking availability prior to issuing other permits or inconveniencing other
businesses.
Changes to Chapter 10.60.010, Signed time limitation.  The City of Cheyenne Ordinance will not allow shifting
of parked vehicles. Therefore, vehicles will no longer be allowed to hopscotch within time limit zones.  An
individual will only be able to park on a block face once a day.  This will encourage individuals working
downtown to use the parking facilities and allow customers to park at businesses store fronts.  The Cheyenne
Police Department will utilize new license plate recognition (LPR) technology mounted on parking vehicles to
monitor compliance with this regulation and ensure that parking space utilization adheres to the posted policy.
The LPR technology will have the ability to monitor space occupancy within designated zones and, based upon
the proposed amendment, vehicles will be required to move to a different block face.  This is a change to move
their vehicle 150 feet which confused the public.
7.
Development of a strong parking maintenance program with regularly scheduled facility
condition appraisals, the creation of parking facility maintenance reserves.
8.
Development of an on-going and collaborative relationships with the MPO, the State, the County
and other local institutions.
9.
Over time, expand the parking program’s mission to adopt a broader more “access management”
oriented perspective.
2
