### Page 15 of 16

```text
Changes to Chapter 10.52.010, Method of parking, Chapter 10.52.120, Diagonal parking permits, and Chapter
10.52.020, Prohibited in specified places.  The Cheyenne Police Department recommends these changes for
safety reasons.  A person backing into a diagonal parking space would cause the person to go against the flow
of traffic and increase the risk of a crash.  Also, the parking of a semi-tractor trailer downtown, other than
loading or unloading, creating a low visibility zone.
Other proposed adaptations to the Municipal Code include an overall reorganization of Chapter 10.12.160,
Authority to impound illegally parked vehicles, Chapter 10.12.170, Immobilization, Chapter 10.16.080, Citation
on illegally parked vehicles, Chapter 10.16.090, Failure to comply with civil parking citation on parked vehicle,
Chapter 10.16.140, Request for hearing in parking cases, Chapter 10.16.150, Parking violation hearings and
appeal procedures, Chapter 10.60.030, Notice of violation, Chapter 10.60.040, Fine schedule and Chapter
10.16.160, Operator error.   These changes explain the new procedures to clarify what constitutes a citation,
what recourse citizens have to contest the citation, procedures to contest, and what are the procedures for
individuals that refuse to pay their citations.  The procedure to correct human or computer errors.  These
ordinance changes will provide some relief to the court as these citations will be civil in nature and not criminal.
In the future, the Parking Administration Manager will be maintaining all parking citations and will be able to
receive payments.  The court will only need to interact with individuals that want to contest the citation.
Another benefit, we will be able to send unpaid parking citations to collections.  This will stimulate individuals
into satisfying their obligations to the City of Cheyenne.  Citizens will be afforded the opportunity to receive a
ten-dollar discount for paying their citation within five days.  We are creating an atmosphere that paying your
obligations in a timely manner has its rewards.  It should be noted that there are almost one-thousand unpaid
parking tickets since the first of January.  This trend is extremely disturbing and inequitable to the individuals
that follow the law.
The final amendments to Chapter 10.64.010, City traffic engineer to designate curb loading zones and Chapter
10.68.030, Procedures for establishment.  These changes are clerical changes to indicate the office that is now
administering parking.
If the proposed amendments are approved by City Council, staff intends to develop a strategic information
campaign that includes public notice, signage and messaging, coordinated outreach with the commercial
businesses and a proactive warning campaign. Our city parking vehicles are now equipped with technology that
allows for a warning notice to be issued in lieu of a parking citation. In coordination with this public outreach
and education campaign, the advanced technology also provides verification if previous warning notices have
been issued by location or violation. This can be an effective tool in sharing the information and educating those
parking in the City of Cheyenne.
The proposed code changes are a comprehensive and foundational approach to improve the parking experience
in the City of Cheyenne.  The amended ordinance still provides City Council oversight and prepares the City for
the future by providing the flexibility and options to adapt for a growing and evolving community.
LEGAL ANALYSIS
This ordinance adoption to amend the City of Cheyenne parking ordnances has been reviewed by the City
Attorney’s Office and approved as to form and language.
3
```

